3526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nHow many ways are there to put 4 balls in 3 boxes if the balls are distinguishable but the boxes are not?\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the problem of determining how many ways there are to put 4 distinguishable balls into 3 indistinguishable boxes, we need to consider the different distributions of the balls among the boxes. Since the boxes are indistinguishable, we only need to count the distinct distributions of the balls.\n\nLet's denote the balls as \\( B_1, B_2, B_3, B_4 \\). We will consider all possible partitions of the set \\(\\{B_1, B_2, B_3, B_4\\}\\) into up to 3 non-empty subsets (since we have 3 boxes).\n\n1. **All 4 balls in one box:**\n   - There is only 1 way to do this: \\(\\{\\{B_1, B_2, B_3, B_4\\}\\}\\).\n\n2. **3 balls in one box and 1 ball in another box:**\n   - We need to choose which ball is alone. There are 4 ways to choose which ball is alone, and the remaining 3 balls will be in one box.\n   - The number of ways is \\(\\binom{4}{1} = 4\\): \\(\\{\\{B_1\\}, \\{B_2, B_3, B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_2\\}, \\{B_1, B_3, B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_3\\}, \\{B_1, B_2, B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_4\\}, \\{B_1, B_2, B_3\\}\\}\\).\n\n3. **2 balls in one box and 2 balls in another box:**\n   - We need to choose 2 balls out of 4 to be in the first box, and the remaining 2 will be in the second box. The number of ways to choose 2 balls out of 4 is \\(\\binom{4}{2} = 6\\).\n   - However, since the boxes are indistinguishable, each partition is counted twice (once for each order of the two boxes). Therefore, we divide by 2.\n   - The number of ways is \\(\\frac{\\binom{4}{2}}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\\): \\(\\{\\{B_1, B_2\\}, \\{B_3, B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_1, B_3\\}, \\{B_2, B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_1, B_4\\}, \\{B_2, B_3\\}\\}\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"92fa698062c6f92b04cc0e27e94c44702ae28276705e09d2298a87ee84ea6fef"},"seconds":23.407095432281494,"seed":393608842436103089,"stop":"eos","text":"4. **2 balls in one box and 1 ball each in the other two boxes:**\n   - We need to choose which 2 balls will be together. There are \\(\\binom{4}{2} = 6\\) ways to choose which 2 balls will be together.\n   - The remaining 2 balls will each be in their own box. Since the boxes are indistinguishable, we do not need to consider the order of the last two boxes.\n   - The number of ways is \\(6\\): \\(\\{\\{B_1, B_2\\}, \\{B_3\\}, \\{B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_1, B_3\\}, \\{B_2\\}, \\{B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_1, B_4\\}, \\{B_2\\}, \\{B_3\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_2, B_3\\}, \\{B_1\\}, \\{B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_2, B_4\\}, \\{B_1\\}, \\{B_3\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_3, B_4\\}, \\{B_1\\}, \\{B_2\\}\\}\\).\n\nAdding up all the possibilities from the cases above:\n- Case 1: 1 way\n- Case 2: 4 ways\n- Case 3: 3 ways\n- Case 4: 6 ways\n\nThe total number of ways to distribute the balls is \\(1 + 4 + 3 + 6 = 14\\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{14}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{14}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"4. **2 balls in one box and 1 ball each in the other two boxes:**\n   - We need to choose which 2 balls will be together. There are \\(\\binom{4}{2} = 6\\) ways to choose which 2 balls will be together.\n   - The remaining 2 balls will each be in their own box. Since the boxes are indistinguishable, we do not need to consider the order of the last two boxes.\n   - The number of ways is \\(6\\): \\(\\{\\{B_1, B_2\\}, \\{B_3\\}, \\{B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_1, B_3\\}, \\{B_2\\}, \\{B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_1, B_4\\}, \\{B_2\\}, \\{B_3\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_2, B_3\\}, \\{B_1\\}, \\{B_4\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_2, B_4\\}, \\{B_1\\}, \\{B_3\\}\\}, \\{\\{B_3, B_4\\}, \\{B_1\\}, \\{B_2\\}\\}\\).\n\nAdding up all the possibilities from the cases above:\n- Case 1: 1 way\n- Case 2: 4 ways\n- Case 3: 3 ways\n- Case 4: 6 ways\n\nThe total number of ways to distribute the balls is \\(1 + 4 + 3 + 6 = 14\\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).","id":"counting_and_probability:412"}
